# Apupiwe: reference
<!-- Generated from spec/reference.toml by tools/spec.py build. Edit the spec, not this file. -->

<span id="r-0-1"></span>This document states what the language is. The reasons for each part are in [rationale.md](rationale.md), its history is in [design-log.md](design-log.md), and the word lists are in the [data folder](https://github.com/LilijoySkyseeker/apupiwe/tree/main/data).

<span id="r-0-2"></span>The language calls itself **apu pi we** "everyone's language", said [ˈapu pi we], roughly AH-poo pee weh, with only the first syllable stressed. Its name in English and other languages is one word, Apupiwe, said the same way. Any language is named with **apu pi** followed by whose language it is.

<span id="r-0-3"></span>Apupiwe is a designed language for people who speak it natively, within the world's existing cultures. Its own grammar is built for native speakers. Where it meets a culture's conventions, such as clocks, calendars, units, names and customs, it carries them. Where conventions conflict and one must be chosen, it follows the international standard.

<span id="r-0-4"></span>What the language is for, and how its criteria are weighed against each other, is set out in [the goal](goal.md).

---

## 1. Principles

1. <span id="r-1-1"></span>The common case costs nothing. Statements, ordinary belief, an unknown source, the topic, and present or unspecified time are unmarked. Frequent meanings have the shortest words.
2. <span id="r-1-2"></span>Heads come first, and position carries role. The order is subject, verb, object. A content word that describes a noun or a verb follows it. Words never move to show their role.
3. <span id="r-1-3"></span>Operators come before what they cover (section 6).
4. <span id="r-1-4"></span>Heavy goes last. A noun's own modifiers come before its **pi** "of" phrase, and long clauses go to the end. Nothing may follow a relative clause in its phrase.
5. <span id="r-1-5"></span>Doubling widens or strengthens any word except a digit in a number: **su su** emphatic "not", **wu wu** extra deference, **yo yo** emergency, **genso genso** "the whole turn is not literal", **nen nen** "each other" (a set phrase), **feko feko** "exactly so". Doubled **su** is always emphasis and never means "not not".
6. <span id="r-1-6"></span>Emphasis, politeness, respect and stance have words of their own: **ka** and **ten** for emphasis, **wu** for politeness, **lanno** for respect, and the stance words of section 7.
7. <span id="r-1-7"></span>A precise way always exists, but it need not always be used. Anything the listener can supply may be left out (section 4.5), and any ambiguity can be removed by saying more.

## 2. Sounds

- <span id="r-2-1"></span>The vowels are a e i o u. In the reference pronunciation, every syllable keeps its full vowel.
- <span id="r-2-2"></span>There are 13 consonants: p b t d k g · m n · f s · l w y. They are made at the lips (p b m w, and f with lip and teeth), with the tongue tip (t d n s l), with the tongue body (y) and with the back of the tongue (k g). In the reference pronunciation p t k are aspirated at the start of a word, with a puff of air as in English *pin*, and b d g are voiced, so the pairs differ both ways: speakers of languages that tell them apart by voicing and those that tell them apart by aspiration both hear the difference. Unaspirated p t k are a harmless variant.
- <span id="r-2-3"></span>A syllable has the shape (C)V(n). A syllable with no consonant before its vowel (as in **apu**, **ewo**) occurs only at the start of a word. The only consonant that can end a syllable is n.
- <span id="r-2-4"></span>Words fall into three types by sound:
  - grammar words have one unstressed syllable (table in section 13), except the two-syllable operators listed after that table, which are stressed on the first like content words;
  - the digits 0–5 have one stressed syllable;
  - every other word has two or more syllables and is stressed on the first.
- <span id="r-2-5"></span>Words never change form. There are no endings, no agreement, no case and no gender.
- <span id="r-2-6"></span>Stress is a higher pitch and a little more force (loudness) on the stressed syllable, stepping down after it. The force keeps stress audible where pitch is lost, as in a whisper or a song. In the reference pronunciation stress does not lengthen the syllable, and the rhythm is syllable-timed.
- <span id="r-2-7"></span>Across a phrase, each stressed syllable is a little lower than the one before (declination), and pitch resets after a pause.
- <span id="r-2-8"></span>There are no tones. Pitch carries only intonation and emotion. A question needs no rising tone, because its words make it a question, but rising anyway is fine.
- <span id="r-2-9"></span>These accent variants are harmless: they never turn one word into another. p t k with or without the puff of air; b d g softened between vowels in an unstressed syllable, as in Spanish *cada*; k and g sounding like "ch" and "j" before i and e; l dark, or as a tap as in Spanish *pero*; f made with both lips; w made with the lip and teeth, as in Dutch *wat*; s as "sh"; a vowel plus n as a nasal vowel; and a catch in the throat (a glottal stop) where a word ending in a vowel meets one starting with a vowel. The reference pronunciation uses the plain sounds, keeps every vowel full, and doesn't lengthen stressed syllables, but none of that is a rule.
- <span id="r-2-10"></span>**mm** "heard you", **huh?** "say again", and any other interjection or hesitation sound speakers use are outside the sound system and carry no grammar.
- <span id="r-2-11"></span>Native content words never begin with an open syllable identical to a digit (**mu ni sa de wo ma**). Loans are exempt.
- <span id="r-2-12"></span>No two content words, and no two short words (grammar words and digits) that can stand in the same place, differ only by sounds a harmless variant (2.9) brings together: b and w, d and l, g and w before o and u, g and y or k and s before i and e. So a speaker with any of these accents is understood, and no contrast hangs on the sound changes these variants begin. A new grammar word also never differs only by its vowel from a grammar word that can stand in its place, since unstressed vowels weaken.

## 3. Writing

- <span id="r-3-1"></span>The letters are a b d e f g i k l m n o p s t u w y, one per sound, with no accents. Digits never appear inside a word. Spelling follows the standard pronunciation, and neither the harmless variants nor stress are written.
- <span id="r-3-2"></span>Lowercase is the standard. Capitals carry no meaning and are harmless.
- <span id="r-3-3"></span>These marks carry grammar:

  | Mark | Use |
  |---|---|
  | `,` | a pause (section 4.6) |
  | `.` | end of a sentence; between digits, the decimal point |
  | `" "` | around quoted speech |

  `?` and `!` are allowed. In the reference variety the words already show what they would mark, so they carry no grammar of their own. `-` and `:` are not used in the language's own words.
- <span id="r-3-4"></span>Some things pass through as written: chat abbreviations and interjections (ok, lol), digits, unit symbols (`5 km`, `20 °C`, `30%`), the local clock notation (`21:30`, `9:30 pm`), ISO dates (`2026-10-10`), letters used as labels, links, email addresses, @handles, #tags, emoji, code, file names, and personal and brand names (section 11). Unit symbols are read aloud by their borrowed names (9.3) and clock notation in the speaker's clock style (8.3). Other notation is read as the speaker's culture reads it.
- <span id="r-3-5"></span>Ordinary writing uses digits for numbers. This reference spells numbers out only to show how they are said. Digits are grouped in threes from the decimal point with a space (`1 250 000`), and `.` is the decimal point.
- <span id="r-3-6"></span>Words are written apart. A phrase said fast keeps its spaces until a fused form actually changes sound and becomes a new word.

## 4. The sentence

### 4.1 Template

<span id="r-4-1-1"></span>A sentence fills these slots in this order. Only the verb, or a fragment (4.5), is required.

<span id="r-4-1-2"></span>

| # | Slot | Contents | Section |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Calling someone | a person word (a name, a title, or a single role or kin word), then a pause | 10.1 |
| 2 | Opener | a linker to the previous sentence (**bo yin** "so"), then a relation phrase that sets the scene (time, place) or a topic **pi X**, then an **if**/**when** clause, each followed by a pause | 4.3 |
| 3 | Speech act | one particle; **genso** or **ka** may come before it | 7.1 |
| 4 | Politeness | **wu**, after the particle or in its place | 10.2 |
| 5 | Clause operators | confidence **pe** + number, source **wanme**/**inwun**/**wemen** | 6.1 |
| 6 | Subject | a noun phrase (section 5) | |
| 7 | Phrase operators, habit, aspect | e.g. **su**, **pon**, **manni**, **ben** | 6.2, 8.1 |
| 8 | Verb | one verb, or verb + verb; with no object, a bare word right after the verb says how | 4.2 |
| 9 | Objects | recipient, then thing | 4.2 |
| 10 | Relation phrases | **no**, **len**, **don**, **nan**, **fan**, **fun** … + noun phrase or clause; **fun** + a word says how | 4.4, 4.2 |
| 11 | Trailing clause | **me**, **bo**, **sin**, **pu** … clause | 4.4 |
| 12 | Stance | a pause, then **bi**, **yan**, **buni**, **donon** … | 7.2 |
| 13 | Calling someone | a pause, then a person word | 10.1 |

### 4.2 The verb and its objects

- <span id="r-4-2-1"></span>For any content word, what you mean decides the clear form:
  - for a state ("is big", "is closed"), use **gun** + word: **guyi gun tugu** "the house is big". A feeling named by an action word is said with the plain verb: **to nuta** "I'm afraid" (I fear);
  - for an event ("walks", "is walking", "walked"), use the word as the verb, with an aspect word when precision matters: **ewo li tedo** "the person is walking", **ewo manni tedo** "the person (usually) walks", **ewo ben tedo** "the person walked";
  - for a description of a noun ("the person who walks", "the house that is big"), use **gi**: **ewo gi tedo**, **guyi gi gun tugu**.
- <span id="r-4-2-2"></span>A bare noun followed by a word (**guyi tugu**, **ewo tedo**) may be a description or a statement. Context decides, and the clear forms above remove any doubt.
- <span id="r-4-2-3"></span>Any content word can take any role by its position. As an object, or with a derivation marker, it is a noun (**mosu** "apologise / an apology").
- <span id="r-4-2-4"></span>How something is done is said with **fun** "like", an ordinary relation phrase after the verb and its objects: **nawi pasu fun yobe** "eat food quickly". A bare word right after an object describes the object: **nawi pasu yobe** "eat fast food". With no object, a bare word right after the verb describes the action: **oyi tesin** "say calmly".
- <span id="r-4-2-5"></span>With two objects, the recipient comes first and then the thing: **wiwa sen nuta** "teach you fear".
- <span id="r-4-2-6"></span>When one verb follows another and the first takes an object (want, try, need, must, can, start, stop, teach, see, like, help…), the second verb is that object: **walo alun** "try to break". Otherwise the second verb describes the first: **doyu pume** "sit thinking", **to isi yinmi tanke** "I go through the forest" (go passing the forest; there is no word for "through"). Phrases and clauses after a second verb that is an object belong to it, the nearest verb: **momu isi len guyi** "want to go home".
- <span id="r-4-2-7"></span>**tula** "do; make" followed by a result and then an object means "make (it) so": **tula upe yin** "fix this". Followed by an object and then a **fun** phrase saying how, it means "do (it) so": **tula yin fun upe** "do this well".
- <span id="r-4-2-8"></span>A command uses **ti**, or **lo** for a request. In casual speech a bare verb with no subject is also a command (**obu!** "come!"); **ti** is the precise form, and "don't" always needs it: **ti su kusa** "don't leave". Otherwise a sentence without a particle is a statement.

### 4.3 Openers

- <span id="r-4-3-6"></span>A linker is a word or phrase at the start that ties the sentence to the one before it, such as **bo yin** "so", **won** "but" or **nun** "and also".
- <span id="r-4-3-1"></span>Any relation phrase may open the sentence, followed by a pause. A time or place phrase there sets the scene: **no minfi, …** "at noon, …". An opening phrase changes no roles.
- <span id="r-4-3-2"></span>A topic is **pi** + a noun phrase at the start, meaning "as for X": **pi tale la, win kuwi guyi, yidu bun la.** "As for the cars, the caretaker takes them." At the start, **pi** has no other reading, and the topic runs to its pause, or to **kiyu** "end of phrase", so it can be a list: **pi upe nun yomo,** "as for good and bad,".
- <span id="r-4-3-3"></span>An if/when clause is **sin** "if", **pu** "while/when", **kon** "after", **ya** "before" or **bo** "because", followed by a clause.
- <span id="r-4-3-4"></span>When several of these open a sentence, they come in this order: linker, scene or topic, if/when clause. The speech-act particle follows them.
- <span id="r-4-3-5"></span>Only a person word (4.1) calls someone. A longer noun phrase before a pause is the subject (4.6), and a fronted relation phrase or **pi** phrase is a scene or topic phrase.

### 4.4 Clauses and relation phrases

- <span id="r-4-4-1"></span>A relation phrase (**no**, **len**, **don**, **nan**, **fan**, **ya**, **kon**, **fun**, **pu**, **bo** "because of", and the two-syllable **anko** "until" and **nuwen** "without") at clause level attaches to the clause's verb. A relation phrase right after a noun may instead describe that noun: **osa nan tanun tufun** "tea with lemon" (not after a pronoun, and not **fun**, which after an object says how). To be precise, use a relative clause for the noun (**osa gi ibe tanun tufun**, **sola gi gun no fide pi yolan** "the road that is above the river") or put the phrase first for the verb (**nan tanun tufun, to koye osa**). In comparisons and quantities (9.3), **don**, **len** and **fun** belong to their number or adjective. After a time, **kon** and **ya** belong to that time (8.2): **to isi no wuma kon dili ni** "I go the day after tomorrow". **bo** with a noun phrase gives **bo yu** "why" and **bo yin** "so" (because of this).
- <span id="r-4-4-2"></span>A relation word may drop its object when context supplies it, as in **kon** "after that" and **ya** "beforehand". It then ends its clause or comes before a pause.
- <span id="r-4-4-3"></span>A relation word can take a clause (**kon mon la yidu ewo kin** "after they took someone") or an action noun (**don kusa** "from leaving").
- <span id="r-4-4-4"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun before it. A time, reason or condition clause at the *start* of a sentence belongs to the main clause. At the *end*, it belongs to the innermost clause before it, unless a pause closes that clause first (**kiyu** doesn't, 5.3): **pu leso, to ben miya ewo gi sigo** "in the morning, I watched the person who came back"; **to ben miya ewo gi sigo pu leso** "I watched the person who came back in the morning". In the same way, a phrase or clause after verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **won** belongs to the last of them, and at the start it covers them all: **mon doku nun nawi no guyi** "he works, and eats at home"; **no guyi, mon doku nun nawi** "at home, he works and eats".
- <span id="r-4-4-5"></span>**nun** "and", **lu** "or" and **won** "but" join nouns, verbs, modifiers or clauses. Nouns in a list are joined with **nun**: **mole nun sine** "mother and father". **lu X lu Y** means "either X or Y, not both".
- <span id="r-4-4-7"></span>Verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **won** are separate predicates. Operators and aspect words before one of them cover only that verb, and an object after the last verb belongs to it: **mon su nawi nun fimo** "she doesn't eat, and she sleeps"; **to nagu nun yidu bun** "I see (it) and take it".
- <span id="r-4-4-6"></span>A quote is framed by a say-verb, and in writing it goes in quotation marks. It is its own sentence with its own speech-act particle. Broken-off speech just stops.

### 4.5 Leaving things out

- <span id="r-4-5-1"></span>Anything the listener can supply from context may be left out: the subject, the object, the object of a relation word or the verb. An answer or follow-up can be just the new part: **nufi yu? moti?** "what time? again?"; **nagu sen la no dili mena ufo.** "see you all Saturday".
- <span id="r-4-5-2"></span>**gun** with no subject is "(it) is", like any verb with its subject left out: **gun nasi** "(it's) money". With **gun** left out too, a bare state is fine: **ten upe.** "very good." "There is" is **ibe** "have" with no subject (4.7).
- <span id="r-4-5-3"></span>When leaving something out could be unclear, say it.
- <span id="r-4-5-4"></span>An action word with no object is read by its subject. A subject that can't act undergoes the action: **beya ben tapi** "the door closed", **fileni ben alun** "the cup broke". A subject that can act does it to something the context supplies: **fo mara ben ibe fileni. mon ben alun.** "Mara had a cup. She broke it." To be precise, say the object, or use **nen** "self".

### 4.6 Pauses

<span id="r-4-6-1"></span>A pause, written as a comma, goes wherever a phrase could otherwise run on. It belongs in these places:
- <span id="r-4-6-2"></span>after someone called at the start, and before someone called at the end;
- <span id="r-4-6-3"></span>after an opener (linker, scene, topic, if/when clause);
- <span id="r-4-6-4"></span>after a subject that contains a clause or a phrasal derived word, before its verb (**win sanwa gi … ben pofi, kifa unfu …**);
- <span id="r-4-6-5"></span>between a recipient and the thing, where it helps: it is optional, and recommended when the thing is several words long or has a relative clause;
- <span id="r-4-6-6"></span>between clauses joined by **nun**, **lu** or **won** when the first ends in a noun, and the same between joined verbs (4.4.7): **to nawi pasu, nun fimo** "I eat food, and sleep";
- <span id="r-4-6-7"></span>before a stance word, and before the choice tag **yu** (4.8);
- <span id="r-4-6-8"></span>between a larger and a smaller calendar unit (**dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli**);
- <span id="r-4-6-9"></span>after a relative clause, to close it before more material (or **kiyu**, 5.3).

<span id="r-4-6-10"></span>In casual writing, pauses that context makes obvious may be dropped.

### 4.7 "Is": gun

- <span id="r-4-7-1"></span>**gun** goes before a state: **guyi gun tugu** "the house is big". Without it, **guyi tugu** is normally read as "the big house".
- <span id="r-4-7-2"></span>**gun** goes before a noun: **mon gun tiwu** "she is a child".
- <span id="r-4-7-3"></span>**gun** goes before a place phrase: **mon gun no guyi** "he is at home".
- <span id="r-4-7-4"></span>**gun** before an action word gives the state of whoever undergoes the action: **gun tapi** "is closed", **mon gun kuwi** "she is protected", **to gun nuta** "I am feared". This holds for every action word, feelings included; the one who feels takes the plain verb (**to nuta** "I'm afraid").
- <span id="r-4-7-5"></span>**gun** before a **me** clause means "is that …".
- <span id="r-4-7-6"></span>**gun** with no subject is always "is" or "to be", with the subject understood: **momu gun tiwu** "want to be a child", **dan ibe sogan fan gun kuwi** "everyone has the right to be protected", **mon obu nun gun simi** "she came and is sick".
- <span id="r-4-7-7"></span>"There is" is **ibe** "have" with no subject, and **ibe** with no subject always means "there is": **ibe pasu** "there's food", **su ibe pasu** "there's no food", **won, ibe yefi fefa** "but there are many things", **sin ibe pasu** "if there is food". To say someone has it, name them: **to ibe pasu** "I have food".

### 4.8 Questions and answers

- <span id="r-4-8-1"></span>A yes/no question starts with **bi**. A **bi** at the end of a clause is a tag, "…, right?".
- <span id="r-4-8-2"></span>Question words stay where the answer goes and need no **bi**: **sen momu me to oyi yu.** "you want me to say what?"
- <span id="r-4-8-3"></span>Question words are built from **yu**: **yu** what; **ewo yu** who; **X yu** which X; **no fela yu** where; **no nufi yu** when; **bo yu** why; **fun yu** how; **wifu yu** how many/much; **pi ewo yu** whose. **wifu yu** follows its noun the way a number does: **ewo wifu yu** "how many people".
- <span id="r-4-8-4"></span>To offer a choice, ask **bi … lu …** "either?", or ask **… lu …, yu** "which?" with a pause before **yu** so it isn't read as "which X". That second kind is answered by echoing the choice.
- <span id="r-4-8-5"></span>An indirect question starts its **me** clause with **bi**, with or without a question word: **to su loya me bi mon obu** "I don't know whether she's coming", **to su loya me bi ewo yu obu** "I don't know who is coming". Without **bi**, a question word anywhere in the sentence makes the whole sentence a question: **sen momu me to oyi yu** "you want me to say what?"
- <span id="r-4-8-6"></span>Answers echo the verb, since there are no words for yes and no: "Aren't you coming?" → **obu** / **su obu**. A question with **gun** is answered by echoing what follows **gun**: "Is the house big?" → **tugu** / **su tugu**. The short answer is **feko** "true" or **su feko** "not true": it says whether the question's statement, exactly as asked, is true, so after a negative question **feko** agrees with the negative: **bi sen su obu** "aren't you coming?" → **feko** "right, I'm not". The echo is the precise answer. To acknowledge, **mm** says you heard, an echo says you understood, and **du** + verb says "will do". **ok** agrees to a proposal or instruction ("let's go" → **ok**) and doesn't answer a yes/no question.
- <span id="r-4-8-7"></span>A lost short word usually leaves a normal sentence, so nobody notices. When it matters, say it twice: **su su** for a "not" that must get through, a yes/no question ended with the tag **bi** as well (**bi sen obu, bi** "are you coming, are you?"), a warning repeated whole, and the answer echoed back in full (**to obu** "I am coming"). In noise or across a distance, speak this way by default.

## 5. The noun phrase

### 5.1 Template

<span id="r-5-1-1"></span>A noun phrase runs in this order: noun, **wan** (5.2.12), content modifiers, number, **la**, determiner, **pi** phrase, relative clause, as in **guyi tugu sa la yin pi fo mara gi …**. Titles and **lanno** come before the noun (10.1), and phrase operators come before the whole phrase (6.2).

### 5.2 Parts

- <span id="r-5-2-1"></span>Describing words follow the noun: **guyi tugu** "big house". For a clear description, use **gi** (5.3).
- <span id="r-5-2-2"></span>Gender is a modifier, stated once when it matters: **tiwu futa** "daughter", **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
- <span id="r-5-2-11"></span>Describing words after a noun each describe the noun: **to ibe lipo wiwi buda** "I have a red, dark cloth". **tan** between two describing words makes the second describe the first: **to ibe lipo wiwi tan buda** "I have a dark-red cloth". It joins only the word before it and the word after it, and works after **gun** too: **lipo gun wiwi tan buda** "the cloth is dark red".
- <span id="r-5-2-12"></span>**wan** right after a noun or a name adds affection, "dear little": **poka wan** "doggy", **fo mara wan** "dear Mara". It comes before the noun's describing words: **poka wan tugu** "the dear big dog". As a grammar word it also ends a name, so a content word can follow it with no pause: **fo mara wan obu** "dear Mara is coming".
- <span id="r-5-2-3"></span>Noun + noun is used only for gender and for the compounds in `compounds.tsv`. Other relations use **pi** or a relative clause.
- <span id="r-5-2-4"></span>Numbers and times can stand alone as noun phrases: **yawu gun upe** "ten is fine", **wuma gun upe** "tomorrow is fine".
- <span id="r-5-2-5"></span>A noun followed by a number is a count (**dili oma** "seven days"). A noun + **mena** + number is a label (**pata mena sa** "document 2"), and a label also gives a place in order: **bifo mena de** "the third time", **ewo mena ni** "the first person". "Last" is **pi fugi**, "of the end": **bifo pi fugi** "the last time". With a number before **pi** "of" and a noun after it, the phrase is a part of a set: **sa pi ewo de** "two of the three people". Two numbers joined by **pi** are a fraction (9.2.7).
- <span id="r-5-2-6"></span>The plural word **la** is optional. A count or context can show plurality.
- <span id="r-5-2-7"></span>There are no articles. Whether something is known or new is left to context, or shown with **yin**, **fin**, **kin** or a number.
- <span id="r-5-2-8"></span>Determiners (table in section 13) and **mesun** "each" follow the noun: **guyi yin** "this house", **ewo kin** "someone", **yefi kin** "something", **ban nume mesun** "each home". A determiner right after a noun or pronoun belongs to it. So when the thing given is a pronoun or a determiner standing alone, mark the recipient with **len**, the normal form there: **to iko fin len mon** "I told that to him" (**to iko mon fin** is "I told that-him"). A determiner may stand alone as a noun phrase: **we** "all", **fe** "some", **pa** "none". A part of a whole is a determiner or number + **pi** + noun: **ye pi silu la** "some of the photos".
- <span id="r-5-2-9"></span>**pi** "of" links a noun to a noun: **guyi pi fo mara** "Mara's house". Each **pi** attaches to the nearest noun before it, and its phrase is the nearest single noun after it, so a list joined by **nun** or **lu** is outside it: **enpa pi pume nun lite pi dakuwu** is "freedom of thought, and religion". To cover every item, repeat the head: **enpa pi pume nun enpa pi lite pi dakuwu** "freedom of thought and freedom of religion".
- <span id="r-5-2-10"></span>"One's own" is **pi nen**: **to ibe guyi pi nen** "I have my own house". Since a **pi** phrase holds a single noun, two owners of one thing take a relative clause: **guyi gi fo mara nun to ibe** "Mara's and my house".

### 5.3 Relative clauses

<span id="r-5-3-1"></span>A relative clause is **gi** + clause, placed after the noun. The noun fills the role missing from the clause:
- <span id="r-5-3-2"></span>subject: **ewo gi tedo** "the person who walks";
- <span id="r-5-3-3"></span>object: **natun gi sen la yaga** "the papers you put";
- <span id="r-5-3-4"></span>place or time, with the relation word at the end of the clause: **fela gi to nume no** "the place where I live". The precise form keeps a pronoun after the relation word: **fela gi to nume no bun** "the place I live in (it)";
- <span id="r-5-3-6"></span>any other relation, the same way: **wonnun gi to fonsu nan** "the tool I cut with";
- <span id="r-5-3-7"></span>"whose", with **pi** + the pronoun that fits the noun (**pi mon**, **pi bun**, **pi mon la**) inside the clause pointing back to it: **ewo gi guyi pi mon gun tugu** "the person whose house is big", **guyi gi beya pi bun gun tapi** "the house whose door is closed".

<span id="r-5-3-8"></span>If both the subject and the object are missing, the noun is the subject: **ewo gi nagu** "the person who sees". For "the person I see", keep the subject: **ewo gi to nagu**.

<span id="r-5-3-5"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun or pronoun before it, so after a **pi** phrase it describes the **pi** noun. To describe the head instead, put a quality before the **pi** phrase or use a new sentence. A pause, or the word **kiyu** "end of phrase", closes it, along with every relative clause still open: **to ben miya ewo gi sigo kiyu pu leso** "I watched the person who came back, in the morning". **kiyu** is optional; use it where a missed pause could mislead. It closes only the phrases that run on: relative clauses, phrasal derived words (5.4) and topics (4.3). A trailing clause (4.4.4) is closed by a pause.

### 5.4 Derived words

<span id="r-5-4-1"></span>Four markers before a verb or verb phrase name a role in the action:

<span id="r-5-4-2"></span>

| Marker | Role | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **win** | the one who does it | **win febo** "one who answers" |
| **gen** | the thing done or made | **gen iko** "what is told" = news |
| **wa** | the thing used | **wa bubo** "thing for laughing" = a joke |
| **ban** | the place | **ban tedo** "place for walking" = a path |

- <span id="r-5-4-3"></span>A **pi** phrase after a derived word belongs to the last noun inside its base, or to the derived word itself if its base has no noun: **wa kuwi pi to** "my thing for keeping".
- <span id="r-5-4-4"></span>The base may be a verb phrase: **win isi don guyi** "one who goes from home", a traveller. It runs to the end of its phrase, like a relative clause, and a pause or **kiyu** separates it from a following verb or describing word: **wa siten sokun** "a pan" (a thing for cooking flat), **wa siten sokun kiyu tikun** "a hot pan"; without **kiyu**, **tikun** would join the phrase inside. It also ends before a name (**fo** …) or a gender word (**futa**, **lowu**), so **win wiwa fo mara** is "Teacher Mara" and **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
- <span id="r-5-4-5"></span>A person something is done to is **ewo gi gun** + verb: **ewo gi gun wiwa** "a student".
- <span id="r-5-4-6"></span>A noun base is linked with **pi** or a verb: **ban pi natun** "an office", **win obu don yaye** "an alien".
- <span id="r-5-4-7"></span>For opposites, **su** + word means "not X" (**su upe** "not good"), and a separate opposite root names the other pole (**yomo** "bad").


