### 5.1 Template

§5-1-1A noun phrase runs in this order: noun, **wan** (5.2.12), content modifiers, number, **la**, determiner, **pi** phrase, relative clause, as in **guyi tugu sa la yin pi fo mara gi …**. Titles and **lanno** come before the noun (10.1), and phrase operators come before the whole phrase (6.2).

### 5.2 Parts

- §5-2-1Describing words follow the noun: **guyi tugu** "big house". For a clear description, use **gi** (5.3).
- §5-2-2Gender is a modifier, stated once when it matters: **tiwu futa** "daughter", **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
- §5-2-11Describing words after a noun each describe the noun: **to ibe lipo wiwi buda** "I have a red, dark cloth". **tan** between two describing words makes the second describe the first: **to ibe lipo wiwi tan buda** "I have a dark-red cloth". It joins only the word before it and the word after it, and works after **gun** too: **lipo gun wiwi tan buda** "the cloth is dark red".
- §5-2-12**wan** right after a noun or a name adds affection, "dear little": **poka wan** "doggy", **fo mara wan** "dear Mara". It comes before the noun's describing words: **poka wan tugu** "the dear big dog". As a grammar word it also ends a name, so a content word can follow it with no pause: **fo mara wan obu** "dear Mara is coming".
- §5-2-3Noun + noun is used only for gender and for the compounds in `compounds.tsv`. Other relations use **pi** or a relative clause.
- §5-2-4Numbers and times can stand alone as noun phrases: **yawu gun upe** "ten is fine", **wuma gun upe** "tomorrow is fine".
- §5-2-5A noun followed by a number is a count (**dili oma** "seven days"). A noun + **mena** + number is a label (**pata mena sa** "document 2"), and a label also gives a place in order: **bifo mena de** "the third time", **ewo mena ni** "the first person". "Last" is **pi fugi**, "of the end": **bifo pi fugi** "the last time". With a number before **pi** "of" and a noun after it, the phrase is a part of a set: **sa pi ewo de** "two of the three people". Two numbers joined by **pi** are a fraction (9.2.7).
- §5-2-6The plural word **la** is optional. A count or context can show plurality.
- §5-2-7There are no articles. Whether something is known or new is left to context, or shown with **yin**, **fin**, **kin** or a number.
- §5-2-8Determiners (table in section 13) and **mesun** "each" follow the noun: **guyi yin** "this house", **ewo kin** "someone", **yefi kin** "something", **ban nume mesun** "each home". A determiner right after a noun or pronoun belongs to it. So when the thing given is a pronoun or a determiner standing alone, mark the recipient with **len**, the normal form there: **to iko fin len mon** "I told that to him" (**to iko mon fin** is "I told that-him"). A determiner may stand alone as a noun phrase: **we** "all", **fe** "some", **pa** "none". A part of a whole is a determiner or number + **pi** + noun: **ye pi silu la** "some of the photos".
- §5-2-9**pi** "of" links a noun to a noun: **guyi pi fo mara** "Mara's house". Each **pi** attaches to the nearest noun before it, and its phrase is the nearest single noun after it, so a list joined by **nun** or **lu** is outside it: **enpa pi pume nun lite pi dakuwu** is "freedom of thought, and religion". To cover every item, repeat the head: **enpa pi pume nun enpa pi lite pi dakuwu** "freedom of thought and freedom of religion".
- §5-2-10"One's own" is **pi nen**: **to ibe guyi pi nen** "I have my own house". Since a **pi** phrase holds a single noun, two owners of one thing take a relative clause: **guyi gi fo mara nun to ibe** "Mara's and my house".

### 5.3 Relative clauses

§5-3-1A relative clause is **gi** + clause, placed after the noun. The noun fills the role missing from the clause:
- §5-3-2subject: **ewo gi tedo** "the person who walks";
- §5-3-3object: **natun gi sen la yaga** "the papers you put";
- §5-3-4place or time, with the relation word at the end of the clause: **fela gi to nume no** "the place where I live". The precise form keeps a pronoun after the relation word: **fela gi to nume no bun** "the place I live in (it)";
- §5-3-6any other relation, the same way: **wonnun gi to fonsu nan** "the tool I cut with";
- §5-3-7"whose", with **pi** + the pronoun that fits the noun (**pi mon**, **pi bun**, **pi mon la**) inside the clause pointing back to it: **ewo gi guyi pi mon gun tugu** "the person whose house is big", **guyi gi beya pi bun gun tapi** "the house whose door is closed".

§5-3-8If both the subject and the object are missing, the noun is the subject: **ewo gi nagu** "the person who sees". For "the person I see", keep the subject: **ewo gi to nagu**.

§5-3-5A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun or pronoun before it, so after a **pi** phrase it describes the **pi** noun. To describe the head instead, put a quality before the **pi** phrase or use a new sentence. A pause, or the word **kiyu** "end of phrase", closes it, along with every relative clause still open: **to ben miya ewo gi sigo kiyu pu leso** "I watched the person who came back, in the morning". **kiyu** is optional; use it where a missed pause could mislead. It closes only the phrases that run on: relative clauses, phrasal derived words (5.4) and topics (4.3). A trailing clause (4.4.4) is closed by a pause.

### 5.4 Derived words

§5-4-1Four markers before a verb or verb phrase name a role in the action:

§5-4-2

| Marker | Role | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **win** | the one who does it | **win febo** "one who answers" |
| **gen** | the thing done or made | **gen iko** "what is told" = news |
| **wa** | the thing used | **wa bubo** "thing for laughing" = a joke |
| **ban** | the place | **ban tedo** "place for walking" = a path |

- §5-4-3A **pi** phrase after a derived word belongs to the last noun inside its base, or to the derived word itself if its base has no noun: **wa kuwi pi to** "my thing for keeping".
- §5-4-4The base may be a verb phrase: **win isi don guyi** "one who goes from home", a traveller. It runs to the end of its phrase, like a relative clause, and a pause or **kiyu** separates it from a following verb or describing word: **wa siten sokun** "a pan" (a thing for cooking flat), **wa siten sokun kiyu tikun** "a hot pan"; without **kiyu**, **tikun** would join the phrase inside. It also ends before a name (**fo** …) or a gender word (**futa**, **lowu**), so **win wiwa fo mara** is "Teacher Mara" and **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
- §5-4-5A person something is done to is **ewo gi gun** + verb: **ewo gi gun wiwa** "a student".
- §5-4-6A noun base is linked with **pi** or a verb: **ban pi natun** "an office", **win obu don yaye** "an alien".
- §5-4-7For opposites, **su** + word means "not X" (**su upe** "not good"), and a separate opposite root names the other pole (**yomo** "bad").

## 6. Operators

### 6.1 Clause operators

§6-1-1Clause operators start the clause (slot 5) and cover all of it.
- §6-1-2Confidence is **pe** + a number of sixths, the chance in 6 that the statement is true: **pe mu** impossible, **pe ni** unlikely, **pe sa** doubtful, **pe de** even odds, **pe wo** likely, **pe ma** very likely, **pe ufo** certain; **pe ma mimi ma** 5.5 in 6; **pe kin** "maybe, possibly", with no particular chance in mind: **pe kin mon obu** "maybe she's coming". An unmarked statement is believed. An exact chance is a number out of a hundred: **pe de yawu pi loki mon obu** "30% that she's coming".
- §6-1-3Source is optional: **wanme** seen, **inwun** told, **wemen** inferred.
- §6-1-4"Might" and "must" in the sense of *probably* are a matter of confidence: "She must be home" is **pe ma mon gun no guyi**.

### 6.2 Phrase operators

§6-2-1Phrase operators cover the word or phrase right after them, including a phrase that opens the sentence (**ga no loso, …** "only in the community, …"). Before the verb, they cover the predicate, which is the verb with everything after it in its clause. To negate a single phrase, put **su** before that phrase: **mon doku su no guyi** "she works, but not at home".
- §6-2-2**su** not (**to su yidu bun** "I don't/didn't take it"; **su su** emphatic). With **pa** "none" it is still one negation, not two: **to su ibe poka pa** "I don't have any dogs";
- §6-2-3**ga** only; **pon** also; **yon** even (**yon sin** "even if"); **min** focus, "this one, not another" (**min fo tomas ben yidu bun** "*Tomas* took it"; before the verb it insists: **to min unfu** "I *did* pay");
- §6-2-4to add a whole statement ("also, …"), start with **nun**; **pon** before the verb means the subject also does it;
- §6-2-5**konsin** at least; **nedin** almost;
- §6-2-6degree words **ke** more, **ten** very, **tason** rather (**ke tugu** "bigger"; **nasi ke uyo** "less money").
- §6-2-7Before the verb, the order is phrase operators, then **manni**, then aspect, then the verb: **to pon manni li doku**. The one exception is **li su** "still not" (8.1).

## 7. Speech acts and stance

### 7.1 Speech acts (slot 3)

§7-1-1

| Particle | Act |
|---|---|
| **bi** | yes/no question |
| **lo** | request; with another subject, a wish (**lo to nagu** "let me see"; **lo dakuwu kuwi sen** "may God keep you") |
| **ti** | instruction (**ti su** "don't") |
| **fon** | advice; the decision stays with the listener |
| **du** | the speaker (or the group the speaker speaks for) commits: **du din** "we will" |
| **yo** | warning; **yo yo** emergency |
| **ka** | exclamation: surprise or strong feeling |
| **genso** | not literal (irony, joking); **genso genso** the whole turn |

- §7-1-2A statement has no particle. Each sentence has one speech act.
- §7-1-3**genso** or **ka** may modify the act from in front of its particle: **genso bi sen loya** "as if you'd know"; **ka bi** is a surprised or indignant question.
- §7-1-4Someone else's intention is reported (**inwun**) or stated with time words and **pe**; in the reference variety, **du** is for the speaker's own commitment.

### 7.2 Stance (slot 12)

§7-2-1After a pause at the end, a word can comment on the whole sentence, whatever its speech act:
- §7-2-2**bi** "…, right?" (checks);
- §7-2-3**yan** "as you know, you see" (shared ground): **mon ben oyi bun, yan.**;
- §7-2-4**buni** "okay?" (softens, invites agreement): **lo kan isi, buni.**;
- §7-2-5**donon** "it turned out" (against expectation): **mon gun win ibe guyi, donon.**
- §7-2-6A half-remembered "wasn't it?" is low confidence plus the tag: **pe de mon ben oyi bun, bi.** "she said it, I think, didn't she?"
- §7-2-7If two stance words occur, one pause comes before both, and **bi** comes last: **mon ben oyi bun, yan bi.** "she said it, you know, right?"
- §7-2-8Other words used in this position are stance words too. The choice tag **, yu** "which?" (4.8) has its own slot at the end and is not a stance word.

