## Lesson 10: Time

Tense is optional. Say when it happens the first time, and after that stop marking it: **afe, to nawi pasu.** "Yesterday, I ate food." The future works the same way: **wuma, to obu.** "Tomorrow, I'll come." The basic time words are **eda** now, **mefa** today, **wuma** tomorrow and **afe** yesterday.  

Three small words show where an action stands. **ben** means done: **to ben nawi** "I've eaten". **li** means in progress, and also "still": **to li nawi** "I'm eating". **buntu** means about to: **to buntu kusa** "I'm about to leave". **ben** is "done" relative to the time you're talking about, so inside a past story it means "had already": **no nufi fin, mon ben liwon** "by then, she had arrived". 

"Not yet" is "still not", **li su**, the way Mandarin and German say it: **to li su nawi.** "I haven't eaten yet." **su ben** is "didn't": **to su ben nawi.** "I didn't eat." "I was eating" is **ben li**, done and ongoing together: **to ben li nawi.**  

For habits, put **manni** before the verb. On its own it means "usually", as in **to manni isi** "I usually go", and with a past time word it means "used to". For intent, **nan momu** is "on purpose" and **su nan momu** "by accident".  

Before the verb, things go in this order: small operator words, then **manni**, then **ben**, **li** or **buntu**, then the verb. For example, **to pon manni li doku** "I'm also usually working". The one exception is **li su** "still not", from above. 

To count from now, use **kon** and **ya**. **kon nelon ni** "after one week" is next week, and **ya dunan ni** "before one month" is a month ago. Any time can be the starting point: **wuma kon dili ni** "the day after tomorrow". 

The units of time are **dili** day, **nelon** week, **dunan** month and **fawu** year. Hours, minutes and seconds have their own words: **yiwo**, **yuku**, **dati**.  

A length of time is a count: **yiwo de** "three hours". **pu** with a length means "for": **pu dili oma** "for seven days". **bifo** is an occasion: **bifo sa** "twice".  

Clock time works the way your culture does, and both styles are always understood. On a 12-hour clock, say the hour, then the minutes as two digits, then the part of the day if it's needed: **no tuli de mu pi yami** "at 9:30 at night", **no yawu mu sa** "at 10:02". On the hour, say just the hour: **no yawu sa** "at 12". Midnight is **yawu sa pi yami**. The parts of the day are **leso** morning, **minfi** noon, **duwun** afternoon, **lokagi** evening and **yami** night. 

On a 24-hour clock, read the four digits one at a time: **no sa ni de mu** "at 21:30". For "around" a time, put **fun** before it: **no fun minfi** "around noon". In writing, give the time the way your culture writes it.  

Dates are labels with **mena** (lesson 9), and the bigger unit comes first, with a comma between: the month by number, then the day. So **dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli** "October 9". A year is read digit by digit: **fawu mena sa mu sa ufo** "2026". On its own, **dili mena** with a number from 1 to 7 is a weekday, counting Monday as 1: **dili mena de** "Wednesday". For a date without its month, add what it's the day of: **dili mena de pi dunan** "the 3rd of the month".  

Age is time lived. **to nume fawu de yawu** "I have lived 30 years" is how you say you're 30. 

## Lesson 11: Making words you don't have

This is the skill that makes a small vocabulary enough. Four markers turn an action into a noun:  

| | | |
|---|---|---|
| **win** + verb | the one who does it | **win wiwa** teacher, **win wungu** leader |
| **gen** + verb | the thing done or made | **gen iko** news ("what's told") |
| **wa** + verb | the thing used for it | **wa nawi** something to eat with |
| **ban** + verb | the place for it | **ban fimo** bedroom ("place for sleeping") |

The base can be a whole verb phrase: **win isi don guyi** "traveller" ("one who goes from home"). It runs to the end of its phrase, so put a comma, or **kiyu**, before a verb or describing word that follows it: **wa siten sokun kiyu tikun** "a hot pan". 

For the person an action is done to, there's **ewo gi gun wiwa** student ("person who is taught"). To link to a noun, use **pi** or a verb: **ban pi natun** an office ("place of paper"), **win obu don yaye** an alien ("one who comes from the sky").  

For opposites, **su** plus a word gives "not X" (**su upe** "not good"), and many words also have an opposite word of their own (**yomo** "bad"). 

Ready-made compounds, such as **win obu don yaye** "alien" ("one who comes from the sky"), are in the [compounds list](../data/compounds.tsv). 

Things the whole world names the same way are borrowed in their international form, without **fo**: **imeyilu** email, **intelunetu** internet, **dolalu** dollar, **kilometulu** kilometre. You can also borrow any word you need from your own language, the way English borrowed *sushi* and *pizza*. Write it the way it sounds, with the language's letters: **to nawi susi sa** "I ate two sushi". A borrowed word needs no **fo**, and it can even be a verb: **to ben gugalu bun** "I googled it". Add **fo** only if it could be mistaken for a word of the language. 

Computer words are everyday words: search **ditun**, delete **nuton**, share **peton**, copy **gayen**, add **ginni**, download **dunu**, install **yaga**, turn on/off **selon**/**tapi**. 

Or describe it. Don't know "spider"? Say **dife meli pi sesen fefa** "small animal of many legs". Describing is a normal part of speaking it. 

## Lesson 12: Longer sentences

**nun** "and", **lu** "or" and **won** "but" join nouns, verbs, describing words or whole clauses. To list nouns, use **nun**: **mole nun sine** "mother and father". Two nouns side by side don't make a list. **lu** X **lu** Y means "either X or Y, not both": **lu osa lu pasu**.  

**sin** "if", **bo** "because", **pu** "while", **kon** "after" and **ya** "before" can each start a clause. **me** is "that", as in **to pume me …** "I think that …". 

### man clauses

A **gi** clause after a noun describes it, the way "who", "which" and "where" do in English. The noun fills the missing part of the clause. It can be the one doing the action, **ewo gi obu** "the person who comes", or the one it's done to, **natun gi sen la yaga** "the papers you put".   

For a place or time, end the clause with the relation word: **fela gi to nume no** "the place where I live" (place that I live at). 

A **gi** clause runs on until something closes it: a comma in writing, a pause in speech, or the word **kiyu** "end of phrase". **to ben miya ewo gi sigo kiyu pu leso.** "I watched the person who came back, in the morning." Without **kiyu** or the comma, "in the morning" would belong to the coming back. Nothing else in the phrase can come after a closed **gi** clause. **kiyu** closes only the things that run on like this: a **gi** clause, a topic, and a derived word built on a phrase (lesson 11). A clause at the end is closed with a comma.  

### Opening a sentence

A time or place phrase can open the sentence: **no minfi, …** "at noon, …". To set a topic, start with **pi**: **pi tale la, to su loya.** "As for the cars, I don't know." An if or when clause can open it too: **sin sen obu, to gun sogi.** "If you come, I'll be happy."   

When several of these open a sentence, the linker comes first (**bo yin,** "so"), then time, place or topic, then the if or when clause, each followed by a comma. The particle comes after all of them (lesson 4). 

Only a person word at the very start can be someone being called (lesson 8). A longer noun phrase there is the subject, even with a comma after it, and a phrase with a relation word or **pi** is a time, place or topic. 

### Commas

Put a comma where you'd pause: 

- after someone called at the start, and before someone called at the end 
- after anything that opens the sentence 
- after a subject that contains a clause or a derived phrase, before its verb: **ewo gi obu, gun win wiwa.** "The person who's coming is a teacher." 
- between who gets something and the thing given, if it helps; it's optional, and worth it when the thing is long or has a **gi** clause 
- between clauses or verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **won** when the first ends in a noun: **to nawi pasu, nun fimo.** "I eat food, and sleep." 
- before a tone word at the end (lesson 8), and before **yu** when it asks "which?" (lesson 4) 
- between month and day (**dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli**) 
- after a **gi** clause, to close it before more words 

In casual writing you can drop commas that are obvious. 

Where a clause goes changes what it attaches to. At the start, it belongs to the main sentence: **pu leso, to ben miya ewo gi sigo** "in the morning, I watched the person who came back". At the end, it belongs to the innermost clause before it: **to ben miya ewo gi sigo pu leso** "I watched the person who came back in the morning". The full rule is in [section 4.4 of the reference](language.md). 

A quote follows a say-verb and is a full sentence with its own particle: **mon oyi "ti obu."** "She said, 'Come.'" 

## Lesson 13: Can, must, maybe

**nebi** is "can": **to nebi lonfe.** "I can swim." **kifa** is "must", and **wupi** is "need to": **kan wupi isi.** "We need to go." For "should", give advice with **fon**: **fon kan isi.** "We should go." Watch where **su** "not" goes, since it covers what comes right after it: **su kifa** is "don't have to", and **kifa su** is "must not". **efon** is "allow": **sen gun efon nawi** "you may eat" (you are allowed to eat). **fan** with a verb means "in order to": **fan bika** "to buy".   

### How sure you are, in sixths

**pe** with a number says the chance in 6 that the statement is true. It goes before the subject.  

| | |
|---|---|
| **pe mu** | impossible |
| **pe ni** | unlikely |
| **pe sa** | doubtful |
| **pe de** | even odds |
| **pe wo** | likely, probably |
| **pe ma** | very likely |
| **pe ufo** | certain |
| **pe kin** | maybe, with no particular chance in mind |

**pe wo mon obu** "4 in 6 she's coming" means she's probably coming, and **pe kin mon obu** is "maybe she's coming". Half steps work too: **pe ma mimi ma** (5.5 in 6). A plain statement means you believe it, so **pe** is optional. For an exact chance, give it out of a hundred: **pe de yawu pi loki mon obu** "30% that she's coming". 

English "might", "probably" and "must be" (when it means *probably*) are all **pe**. "She must be home" is **pe ma mon gun no guyi**. "Must" meaning *have to* is **kifa**.  

For "if" and "if only", **pe** after **sin** says how likely the condition is: **sin pe ni** "if (unlikely)". **sin pe mu** is "if (but it isn't so)": **sin pe mu to ben isi, to ben nagu mon.** "If I had gone, I would have seen her." There's no word for "would". The second clause is the result. 

As an optional extra, you can say how you know: **wanme** "I saw it", **inwun** "I was told", **wemen** "I worked it out". These go where **pe** goes: **inwun mon kusa.** "She left, I'm told." You can skip them until the rest feels easy.  

## Lesson 14: Putting it together

A full sentence has a fixed order. You'll rarely use every part, and only the verb is required.  

1. someone you're calling, then a comma
2. openers (linker, time or place or **pi** topic, if/when clause), each with a comma
3. the particle (**bi**, **lo**, **ti** …), with **genso** or **ka** in front if needed
4. **wu**
5. **pe** and how you know
6. the subject
7. small operator words, **manni**, then **ben**/**li**/**buntu**
8. the verb (or verb + verb), and when there's no object, a describing word for how
9. who gets it, then what
10. relation phrases (**no**, **len**, **don**, **nan**, **fan**, **fun** …), with how as **fun** + a describing word
11. a clause at the end (**bo**, **sin**, **me** …)
12. a comma, then a tone word (**yan**, **buni**, **donon**, **bi**)
13. a comma, then someone you're calling

This sentence has many of these parts: **fo mara, afe, pe wo mon pon ben obu len ban pi natun, yan.** "Mara, yesterday she probably came to the office too, you know." In order, it has someone called, an opener, **pe**, the subject, **pon**, **ben**, the verb, a relation phrase and a tone word.

