## 2. Sounds

| Decision | Why | How it was made | To change it |
|---|---|---|---|
| Vowels **a e i o u** | [evidence] the most common and most dispersed 5-vowel system (vowel dispersion, Liljencrants & Lindblom 1972) | — | Any vowel change rebuilds all words (`build.py build`), breaking the freeze. |
| 13 consonants **p b t d k g m n f s l w y**; places: lips, tongue tip, tongue body (y), back | [evidence] Miller & Nicely 1955: place of articulation is the cue noise destroys first; voicing and nasality survive to −12 dB | Inventory compared at a 3,000-root scale (`build.py scale`): the inventory must leave room for that many distinct two-syllable words | `build.py` `FULL`. |
| **/v/ and /z/ deleted** | [evidence + Claude] uncommon, not rare: each is in over a quarter of inventories (PHOIBLE: 27% and 30%), the two least common of the candidate consonants; /v/ is confusable with /b/, and /v/ and /w/ merge (mostly w → v: 24 vs 9 in a rough Index Diachronica count); /s/–/z/ merger attested (Spanish), otherwise unsourced; founders' sounds win (Hebrew), so sounds a world founder population lacks are at risk; extending that to "rare sounds" is our inference (Israeli Hebrew kept /v/ and /z/ because its founders had them). *(Corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C3–C5: was "rare", "/v/ drifting toward /w/", "rare sounds are at risk".)* | `build.py scale`: removing /z/ cost almost nothing at 3,000 roots | Re-add in `FULL`; rebuild. |
| Syllables **(C)V(n)**; vowel-only syllables only word-initially; **n** the only coda | [evidence] CV is universal and robust; one coda keeps words short without clusters | — | `Inventory` in `build.py`. |
| No tones | [Claude] | Leaves pitch for intonation and emotion; tones are hard for adult learners (Gate) | — |
| **Stress on the first syllable** of every content word; grammar words unstressed; stress is **higher pitch, not length** | [evidence] fixed initial stress marks word beginnings (Finnish, Hungarian); listeners hear lengthening as word *ends*. The author asked to weigh drift; the drift risk of initial stress (later syllables eroding) is handled by drift-proofing (§3.3) | Challenged by the author ("should stress be fixed?"), kept after research (`research/notes/stress-and-sound-change.md`) | — |
| **Declination**: stressed peaks step down across a phrase, reset at a comma or full stop | [author's reading test] runs of two-syllable words made the voice "bounce" | Specified from how most languages behave in statements; declination is suspended in questions in some (Hausa) and limited in multi-level-tone languages (Yoruba) *(corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C10: was "nearly all languages")* | — |
| Harmless variants declared in advance (k/g → ch/j before i/e, nasal vowels, soft middle consonants) | [evidence] Gurevich 2004: 92% of 230 lenitions merged nothing. Covers the soft middle consonants only: velar palatalisation and nasal vowels aren't lenitions, so their harmlessness rests on our own collision check *(scope added 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C8)* | The vocabulary is built so these never merge two words (§3.3) | — |

