- <span id="r-7-2-8"></span>Other words used in this position are stance words too. The choice tag **, yu** "which?" (4.8) has its own slot at the end and is not a stance word.

## 8. Time

### 8.1 Tense and aspect

- <span id="r-8-1-1"></span>Tense is optional. A time word or the first **ben** of a narrative sets the frame, the time that what follows is about, and later verbs in that frame stay unmarked. The future is shown by a time word or by context: **no wuma, gegi nuwo** "it will rain tomorrow".
- <span id="r-8-1-2"></span>Aspect words come right before the verb. **ben** means done. With no frame set it reads as past. Inside a past frame it marks something already done by that time, "had done", and inside a future frame it is "will have". **li** means ongoing, and also "still". **buntu** means about to. **ben li** is "was doing, had been doing": ongoing before now, or before the time being talked about (**to ben li nawi** "I was eating").
- <span id="r-8-1-3"></span>"Not yet" is **li su** "still not", as in Mandarin *hái méi* and German *noch nicht*: **to li su nawi** "I haven't eaten yet". Here the aspect word comes before **su**, which covers only the verb. **su ben** is "not done, didn't": **to su ben nawi** "I didn't eat".
- <span id="r-8-1-4"></span>**manni** "usually" marks habit. With a past frame it means "used to".
- <span id="r-8-1-5"></span>Intent is marked with **nan momu** "on purpose" and **su nan momu** "by accident".

### 8.2 Time words

- <span id="r-8-2-1"></span>The time roots are **eda** now, **mefa** today, **wuma** tomorrow, **afe** yesterday; **dili** day, **nelon** week (7 days), **dunan** month, **fawu** year; **nufi** time (a span or a point); **bifo** occasion (**bifo sa** "twice").
- <span id="r-8-2-2"></span>A time can be an anchor plus an offset, and the offset names its unit: **wuma kon dili ni** "the day after tomorrow". With no anchor, the offset counts from the current frame (8.1), which is now unless a story has set another time: **kon nelon ni** "a week from now, next week", **ya dunan ni** "a month ago". Any time or event can be the anchor.
- <span id="r-8-2-3"></span>A duration is a count: **yiwo de** "three hours". **pu** + duration means "during, for" a span that frames the clause: **pu dili oma** "for seven days".
- <span id="r-8-2-4"></span>Age is lived time: **to nume fawu de yawu** "I have lived 30 years".
- <span id="r-8-2-5"></span>Hours, minutes and seconds have roots: **yiwo**, **yuku**, **dati**.

### 8.3 Clock, calendar, dates

- <span id="r-8-3-1"></span>The clock follows the speaker's culture, and both styles are always understood.
  - The 24-hour style reads four digits one at a time: **no mu tuli de mu** "at 09:30", **no sa ni de mu** "at 21:30".
  - The 12-hour style counts the hour (1–12), then gives the minutes as two digits, then optionally **pi** + a part of the day: **no tuli de mu pi yami** "at 9:30 at night"; **no yawu mu sa** "at 10:02". On the hour, the hour is said alone: **no yawu sa** "at 12".
  - The parts of the day are **leso** morning, **minfi** noon, **duwun** afternoon, **lokagi** evening and **yami** night. Midnight is **yawu sa pi yami**.
  - For *about* a time, put **fun** before it: **no fun minfi** "around noon".
- <span id="r-8-3-2"></span>A weekday is **dili mena** + 1–7 on its own, with Monday as 1 (ISO 8601): **dili mena de** "Wednesday". A day of the month is said with its month; without one, say whose day it is: **dili mena de pi dunan** "the 3rd (of the month)", **dili mena de pi isi** "the third day of the trip".
- <span id="r-8-3-3"></span>Dates are labels (5.2) and put the larger unit first, with a pause between: **dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli** "October 9". A weekday with a time is said the same way: **dili mena ma, no …**. Years are read digit by digit: **fawu mena sa mu sa ufo** "2026".

